        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying an OMA Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these apply to any marketplace treadmill; they matter more where the brand has no US store to fall back on.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the horsepower in the title</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">OMA’s 7200EB is listed at 2.25, 2.5 and 3.0 HP in different places, and a Caroma buyer found 2.25 HP on a motor sold as 3.25. Horsepower in a marketplace title is the least reliable figure. Judge the machine on belt size, capacity, warranty and returns.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a short belt to run on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 45-inch belt is a walking belt. Running on it means a shortened stride and a real risk of stepping off the back. If you want to run, buy a treadmill with at least a 55-inch deck.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not recording the warranty on the day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">OMA’s warranty varies by listing, and listings change. Screenshot the warranty, the specification and the seller’s name when you order, so you can show what you were promised if something fails.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the returns window run out unopened</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill in its box can sit for weeks. Assemble it and test every speed and incline setting inside the returns window, so a fault is a return rather than a warranty claim to a maker you may not be able to reach.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
